{"id":"h207","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"At a certain temperature,Keqfor the reaction 3C_2H_2 \\rightleftarrows C_6H_6 is 4. If the equilibrium concentration of C_2H_2 is 0.5 mole/liter, what is the concentration of C6H_6 ?\nIs the correct answer \"0.5 moles/liter\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The equilibrium concentration of C6H6 is 0.5 mol/L.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The equilibrium expression is K = [C6H6]/[C2H2]^3, with products over reactants raised to stoichiometric powers.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Substituting K=4 and [C2H2]=0.5 gives 4 = [C6H6]/0.125.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Solving gives [C6H6] = 4 × 0.125 = 0.5 mol/L.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"If K were written inversely, [C6H6] would be about 0.031 mol/L.","confidence":0.4,"lean":-1,"theta":0.6283,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The inverse form of K is not the standard convention for a forward-written reaction, so it does not apply.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Someone might forget to cube [C2H2], but using the exponent correctly removes this problem.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The standard convention and correct cubing both give 0.5 mol/L, so the answer is yes.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.95,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":-0.6,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":-0.6,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"}